# A minimal delta on Google, deliberately not a `clang-format --dump-config`.
#
# https://github.com/intensivedatacomp/ALT-CPP -- the earlier, abandoned C++
# attempt -- commits a full dump of every option for one clang-format version.
# That pins the project to the assumptions of the version that produced it: new
# releases add options and occasionally change defaults, so the dump silently
# freezes those defaults and every upgrade produces a large, meaningless diff.
# `BasedOnStyle: Google` plus the genuine overrides expresses the same intent
# and survives upgrades.
#
# The version is pinned in .pre-commit-config.yaml (mirrors-clang-format v18.1.3)
# to match the clang-format-18 that docker/dev.Dockerfile installs. clang-format
# output differs between major versions, so a developer running a different one
# fights an endless reformatting loop. Run pre-commit inside the dev container
# and the two are identical by construction.
---
BasedOnStyle: Google

# Carried over from ALT-CPP: 4 is the house style.
IndentWidth: 4

# 80 columns, as ALT-CPP had. Doc comments and mathematical expressions both
# read better narrow, and it keeps two files side by side on one screen.
ColumnLimit: 80

# `int *p`, not `int* p` -- the star binds to the name, which is what the
# declarator syntax actually means and what keeps `int *p, *q` honest.
PointerAlignment: Right

# Google leaves this true, which tells clang-format to infer pointer alignment
# *per file* from whatever is already there -- so PointerAlignment above would
# be ignored and both spellings could persist in different files. ALT-CPP sets
# PointerAlignment without this and gets exactly that inconsistency.
DerivePointerAlignment: false

# Follows from IndentWidth: 4. Google's -1 is chosen against its own IndentWidth
# of 2, which puts `public:` one column left of the members; at IndentWidth 4
# that same -1 would land it at an arbitrary 3 spaces. -4 keeps the intent --
# access specifiers flush with the `class` keyword.
AccessModifierOffset: -4

# The project is C++20 (CLAUDE.md, and the plan's Conventions section). Google's
# default is Auto, which guesses per file and can misparse a construct it has
# not seen an example of yet.
Standard: c++20

# Braces on their own line, reversing Google's `Attach`. This is the one place
# where a named style says what a `BraceWrapping:` block would take fifteen keys
# to say, and it stays a named style deliberately -- the fifteen keys would be
# the `--dump-config` mistake in miniature, freezing defaults for the wrapping
# options this project has no opinion about.
#
# `Allman` rather than `Stroustrup`: Stroustrup breaks only before function
# definitions, `else` and `catch`, and leaves `if`, `for`, `while` and `switch`
# attached -- which is not what "braces on a new line" means to anyone reading
# the result.
#
# Two things Allman does *not* break, because Google's `AllowShort*` options are
# orthogonal to it and are left alone:
#
#   * short function bodies -- `int size() const { return n_; }` stays on one
#     line (`AllowShortFunctionsOnASingleLine: All`), which is what keeps
#     one-line accessors in a header from costing four lines each;
#   * short lambdas -- `[&](int x) { return x * 2; }` likewise
#     (`AllowShortLambdasOnASingleLine: All`), which is what keeps an STL
#     algorithm call readable.
#
# Set `AllowShortFunctionsOnASingleLine: Empty` and
# `AllowShortLambdasOnASingleLine: Empty` if the rule should be literal instead.
# Note that neither affects a braced `if`/`for` body: those are governed by
# `AllowShortBlocksOnASingleLine`, which Google already sets to `Never`.
BreakBeforeBraces: Allman
